 "fn-47": "<a href=\"https://ja.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E6%9C%A8%E8%B3%83%E5%AE%BF\"><code>kichinyado-jawiki</code></a> - 「their sites, too, increased from the highways into the so-called poor quarters of the urban areas」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「場所も街道から都市部のいわゆる貧民街に増加し」)「They became dens of the poor, where laborers and the unregistered were put to sleep jumbled together in a large common room at about one tatami mat per person.」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「労働者や無宿人を大部屋に一人畳一枚程度で雑魚寝させる貧民の巣窟となった。」) (their sites shifted from the highways to the so-called poor quarters of the cities; they became dens of the poor where laborers and the unregistered slept packed in a common room at about one tatami each. (The registry's firewood-fee passage is also on the page: 「They paid 3 mon in bita coin as the firewood charge, that is, as kichin.」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「薪代すなわち木賃として鐚3文を支払った。」)「As for meals, the rule was that the lodgers contributed ingredients such as rice among themselves and paid an amount equivalent to the firewood cost to have them cooked.」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「食事は宿泊客が米など食材を出しあい、薪代相当分を払って料理してもらうのが原則であった。」)「lodgers stayed in a large common room, and it was not uncommon for bedding, too, to be at their own expense」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「宿泊者は大部屋で、寝具も自己負担が珍しくなく」)))",
 "fn-48": "<a href=\"https://www4.city.kanazawa.lg.jp/material/files/group/22/kanazawajouka1-1-s.pdf\"><code>kanazawa-jokamachi-study</code></a> - 「By this reconstruction of the city, the ordinary retainers' residences were placed between the inner and the outer sogamae, and the temple quarter, the lower mansions and the artisans' town outside the outer sogamae; inside the castle stood the Maeda family's mansion and the various offices, completing a spatial structure of the samurai quarter oriented on a centripetal hierarchy with the Maeda at its apex.」 (translated from the Japanese by this project; original: 「以上の都市改造によって、内惣構と外惣構の間に平士屋敷、外惣構外に寺院街、下屋敷、職人町が配され、城内には前田家の屋敷と諸役所が立地し、前田家を頂点としたヒエラルキーの貫徹した求心性を志向する武家地の空間構造が完成した。」)",
 "fn-49": "<a href=\"https://zdic.net/hans/%E5%85%B3%E5%8E%A2\"><code>guanxiang-zdic</code></a> - 「The streets outside a city gate and the area nearby.」 (translated from the Chinese by this project; original: 「城门外的街道及附近区域。」)「In antiquity, the lodges near a city wall.」 (translated from the Chinese by this project; original: 「古代城郭附近的亭舍。」)「The main street outside a city gate.」 (translated from the Chinese by this project; original: 「城门外的大街。」) (the entry gives the streets, the lodges and the main street outside the gate; a MARKET springing up there is this record's own reading and is on no page read)",
 "fn-50": "<a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Economy_of_the_Song_dynasty\"><code>economy-song-enwiki</code></a> - \"He wrote that 700 such households were acting as iron smelters, with 200 having the most adequate amount of government support, such as charcoal supplies and skilled craftsmen (the iron households hired local unskilled labor themselves).\"",
 "fn-51": "<a href=\"https://donwagner.dk/MingFe/MingFe.html\"><code>wagner-ming-iron</code></a> - \"it includes more than 200 furnace tenders, 300 miners, and 200 'water-carriers' and charcoal producers; transportation is provided by 200 oxen and 50 river vessels.\" The third figure covers two occupations together, so it gives no count for the fuel trade on its own.<!-- feature 238, read against the GM's downloaded copy: the assertion this note was attached to had read the 200 as charcoal producers alone and concluded that the fuel workforce was as large as the furnace workforce. It is not what the source says - the furnace figure is \"more than\" 200, and the 200 is shared. -->",
 "fn-52": "<a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Economy_of_the_Song_dynasty\"><code>economy-song-enwiki</code></a> - \"Historian Robert Hartwell estimates that per capita iron output rose sixfold between 806 and 1078, such that, by 1078 China was producing 127,000,000 kg (125,000 t) in weight of iron per year.\" \"However, historian Donald Wagner questions Hartwell's method used to estimate these figures\"",
 "fn-53": "<a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Economy_of_the_Song_dynasty\"><code>economy-song-enwiki</code></a> - \"In the smelting process of using huge bellows driven by waterwheels, massive amounts of charcoal were used in the production process, leading to a wide range of deforestation in northern China.\" \"However, by the end of the 11th century the Chinese discovered that using bituminous coke could replace the role of charcoal, hence many acres of forested land in northern China were spared from the steel and iron industry with this switch of resources.\"",
 "fn-54": "<a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ton%27ya\"><code>tonya-enwiki</code></a> - \"trade brokers in Japan, primarily wholesalers, warehouse managers, and shipment managers\" \"came to serve a crucial role in the economy of the Edo period (1603 - 1867)\"",
 "fn-55": "<a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fires_in_Edo\"><code>fires-in-edo-enwiki</code></a> - \"When the fires were counted by the month, March saw the most 'great fires', followed by February, April and January. These four months accounted for 70% of all great fires.\" \"It contributed to many winter and spring fires that occurred in dry weather brought about by prevailing northwest and north winds.\" \"inflammable wooden machiya buildings that were heated by charcoal-burning fireplaces\"",
 "fn-56": "<a href=\"https://www.fao.org/4/X5555E/x5555e06.htm\"><code>fao-charcoal-safety</code></a> - \"It has a tendency to absorb the oxygen from the air. Rapid absorption creates considerable heat\" \"Tightly packed masses of charcoal fines and charcoal with a high content of volatiles are more subject to spontaneous combustion\"",
